Context (pp. 7-9, 'Correspondances fonctorielles. Dualité des topos'). For a small category A, A^∧ = Hom(A°,Ens) (presheaves) and A^∨ = Hom(A,Ens) (copresheaves), with Yoneda embeddings h_A: A→A^∧ and h^A: A→A^{∨°}. Page 7 sets up two dual pairings. The first is the Isbell conjugation pair φ_A, ψ_A with pairing H_A(Φ,Ψ) = Hom. The second is the contraction H^A(Φ,Ψ) = Φ*_AΨ, the coend/tensor product of functors, which gives A^∧ ≃ Hom_!(A^∨,Ens) and A^∨ ≃ Hom_!(A^∧,Ens).

This page generalises to several variables and then to topoi.

(1) Multi-variable presheaves. (∏A_i)^∧ = Hom(A_1°×…×A_n°,Ens). For a product topos 𝒳_1×…×𝒳_n with 𝒳_i = presheaf topos on A_i, an object F is a functor A_1°×…×A_n° → Ens. Equivalently it is a functor A_1^{∧°}×…×A_n^{∧°} → Ens that commutes with limits in each variable. The top line, A_1^{∧°}×A_2^∨×A_3×A_4^{∧°}×…, shows he wants mixed-variance versions in which each factor is replaced by its presheaf completion, its copresheaf completion, or itself.

(2) A 'tensor/duality calculus' for topoi. Write X_1⊗X_2 for the product topos and X^∨ for a dual topos. He posits X_1⊗X_2 ≃ Hom(X̌_1,X̌_2;𝟙) ≃ Hom(X_1^∨,X_2) ≃ Hom(X_2^∨,X_1), i.e. a closed, compact-closed-type structure. On sheaf categories this reads:
- 𝓕(X_1×X_2) ≃ Hom^{!!}(𝓕(X_1)°,𝓕(X_2)°;Ens), functors that are limit-preserving in each variable;
- ≃ Hom^!(𝓕(X_1)°,𝓕(X_2)), limit-preserving functors;
- ≃ Hom^!(𝓕(X_2)°,𝓕(X_1));
- and dually ≃ Hom_{!!}(𝓕(X_1)^∨,𝓕(X_2)^∨;Ens) ≃ Hom_!(𝓕(X_1)^∨,𝓕(X_2)), with colimit-preserving functors out of the 'dual' sheaf categories.
For presheaf topoi these are exactly the identifications from p. 7. For general topoi the description Sh(X×Y) ≃ Fun^{lim}(Sh(X)^op, Sh(Y)) is the tensor product of presentable categories, Sh(X)⊗Sh(Y), in Pr^L.

(3) Multi-index version. Take X_I = ∏_{i∈I}X_i, a partition I = ∐_{j∈J} I_j, and a sign partition J = J^+ ⊔ J^-. Then 𝓕(X_I) is identified with:
- multi-limit-preserving functors ∏_J 𝓕(X_{I_j})° → Ens;
- multi-colimit-preserving functors on ∏_J 𝓕(X_{I_j}^∨);
- mixed functors on ∏_{J^-}𝓕(X_{I_j}^∨) × ∏_{J^+}𝓕(X_{I_j})°;
- and finally 'Hom_!(∏_{J^-}…, ∏_{J^+}…)' and 'Hom^!(∏_{J^+}𝓕(X_{I_j})°, ∏_{J^-}𝓕(X_{I_j}^∨))'.
This is a polycategorical bookkeeping of inputs (J^-) and outputs (J^+), i.e. multi-correspondences between topoi.

(4) Morphisms and duality. For X ⇉ Y (f,g) he writes the duals X̌ ⇉ Y̌ (f^∨,g^∨). He asks what 𝓕(X^∨) is ('𝓕(X^∨)?').

(5) Topoi attached to categories. Top(A) := the topos with 𝓕(Top(A)) = A^∨ = Ens^A. Geometric morphisms Top(A)→X correspond to functors A → Pt(X); this is correct, since flatness and continuity into a presheaf topos are pointwise. A correspondence Top(A) →φ X →ψ Top(B) then lets one move objects:
- F ∈ A^∨ is pushed forward by φ̌_* (via the dual);
- G ∈ B^∨ is pulled back by ψ^*.
The next page writes the resulting adjunction formulas ⟨Φ, f^*Ψ⟩_A ≃ ⟨f_!Φ, Ψ⟩_B for the contraction pairing. The aim is a functorial theory of 'correspondences' between small categories realised through topoi, with a duality X ↦ X^∨ extending A^∧ ↔ A^∨.
